  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Virtual Innovation and Support Networks, the seventh volume in Peter Lang's established Telecollaborative Learning and Virtual Exchange in Education series, demonstrates measurable scholarly uptake within a specialized but active research community. Published in 2024 by co-authors Robert O'Dowd and Margarita Vinagre, the book has accumulated 10 citations and 502 downloads, positioning it in the top 10% of cited works for its field and publication year. The work aligns with the sustainable development goal of Industry, Innovation and Infrastructure, reflecting its focus on innovative pedagogical methodologies for online learning environments.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">This open-access scholarly book reports findings from the VALIANT project, a large-scale European initiative exploring how Virtual Exchange\u2014sustained online collaborative learning between students from different cultural backgrounds\u2014can be deployed in teacher education and language learning contexts. The authors, both established scholars in telecollaboration and language education, position the work within a coherent research tradition spanning over a decade of publications on technology-mediated intercultural learning.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record reflects genuine scholarly engagement with the book's core arguments and evidence. Ten citing works have appeared between 2025 and 2026, concentrated in education-focused venues including Language Learning & Technology, Innovation in Language Learning and Teaching, Classroom Discourse, and the European Journal of Education. Co-author Vinagre herself appears as an author on one 2026 citing article on sentiment analysis in virtual exchange, indicating continued development of the research agenda. The citing landscape is geographically distributed, with contributing authors and institutions from Germany (4 citations), Spain (3), Brazil, Switzerland, France, the United Kingdom, Lithuania, and Turkey\u2014a pattern consistent with the transnational nature of virtual exchange research. Citing work types span peer-reviewed articles, book chapters in major handbooks, and reference entries, suggesting the volume serves both as a direct source for new research and as a canonical reference for pedagogical frameworks in the field.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage metrics indicate active engagement beyond the citation record. The book has been downloaded 502 times via the OAPEN platform, with notable uptake in early 2025 and a spike in August 2025 (132 downloads that month). This level of download activity aligns with open-access monograph norms in the education and language learning sectors. The work is catalogued in the Directory of Open Access Books (DOAB) and maintains holdings in at least three library systems globally, supporting its circulation within formal teaching and research environments.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Positioned within a series dedicated to telecollaboration and virtual exchange, the book builds on earlier volumes authored by O'Dowd himself (notably In This Together, 2018; Researching Online Foreign Language Interaction and Exchange, 2012) as well as foundational works by other leading scholars in the field. This serial positioning and the concentration of citations among language and education specialists suggest the work fills a defined niche in a maturing research domain, rather than achieving cross-disciplinary or mainstream visibility. The early citation trajectory\u2014with six citing works in 2025 alone\u2014points to continuing relevance as practitioners and researchers extend virtual exchange methodologies into teacher education and intercultural competence development.</p>",
